

Curvature‑Flux Helix Engine for Magnetorotational Simulation, Exotic Matter Stability, and Cryptographic Provenance

C. Matthew BlackwellIndependent Designer, Lafayette, Louisiana, USA

Abstract

Astrophysical simulations typically treat magnetic field evolution, turbulence, topology, and data provenance as separate computational domains. This work introduces a unified architecture—the Curvature‑Flux Helix Engine—that couples geometric curvature, torsion, and flux density directly to magnetorotational instability (MRI) dynamics, shear‑driven stress transformation, and exotic‑matter stability encoding. The system integrates a cryptographically self‑verifying simulation pipeline using sheared‑key cryptosystems to ensure reproducibility and provenance. Conceptual tests indicate geometry‑dependent MRI zones, stable exotic‑matter candidate pockets, and topological invariants not present in baseline MHD simulations. This architecture proposes a new class of astrophysical simulation capable of predicting field‑shaped matter and generating secure scientific artifacts.

1. Introduction

Accretion disks and magnetized astrophysical plasmas exhibit complex interactions between curvature, torsion, magnetic flux, turbulence, and shear. Standard magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) solvers treat these interactions as weakly coupled, limiting their ability to capture geometry‑driven instabilities and stability pockets.

Simultaneously, scientific reproducibility and data integrity remain major challenges in high‑performance simulation environments. Most pipelines lack cryptographic provenance, leaving results vulnerable to tampering or ambiguous lineage.

This paper presents a unified architecture that addresses both challenges:(1) a simulation engine that couples curvature, flux, and shear directly to MRI physics, and(2) a cryptographic layer that produces self‑verifying scientific artifacts.

2. System Architecture

2.1 Data Ingestion Layer

Raw astrophysical survey data (FITS/HDF5) are ingested and converted into density, pressure, angular velocity, and seed magnetic field fields. Coordinate systems and topology are initialized for curvature‑flux coupling.

2.2 Curvature‑Flux Helix Engine

A geometric backbone is defined through the simulation domain using curvature κ(s), torsion τ(s), and flux density Φ(s). The core relation

[ \frac{dF}{ds} = T(s) ]

links geometric tension to field‑linked force, enabling magnetic field lines to follow helix geometry.

2.3 MRI Solver with Geometry Coupling

The Lorentz force

[ \mathbf{J} \times \mathbf{B} = \frac{dr}{dx} ]

is modified to incorporate helix curvature and torsion. MRI growth rates become functions of κ(s), τ(s), and Φ(s), producing geometry‑dependent instability zones.

